Bloo Kid 2 is a rock solid
. The make or break of any platformer is the controls and this game passes with flying colors.
In addition to completing the level there are 6 additional challanges for each level;

Pluses and cons first:
Plus:
+ The game is pretty. The ships are well designed and seem big as it should be;
+ Gameplay is fun in the beginning, it takes real strategy to win battles. You can't just send them to attack and expect a victory;
+ Cool mechanics makes it really tactical so some decisions might cost you the match or might guarantee a win;
Cons:
- Unbalance is as eternal as the Emperor;
- Bugs that should've been fixed still aren't even though they were noticed during early beta and the game is now gold;
- Campaign starts fun but gets boring really fast since the missions are all the same;
- The studio doesn't really care about its costumers. I bought early adopter edition to support the game and get the free DLC but two days after the early adoption period ended, they gave huge discounts on both the game and DLC, making the early adoption a totally foolish thing and betraying supporters;
- MP is really limited and gets boring fast. Sometimes you get matched with players with huge fleets;
Conclusion:
Buy the game if you're a W40k fan like me but only do so a year from now then the game is with a 75% discount or something. Don't expect to get better also. Devs say they hear the community but some balance changes were never implemented and the DLC not only was released with a lot of bugs but is totally not worthy except for the cosmetic part.

A nice Mario inspired Retro Platformer with the following pros and cons:
+ Nice gameplay;
+ Colourful worlds that remind me of
;
+ Okay boss fights
- Too slow-paced sometimes, especially under water;
- Score system: (1) earn 1 star per level while defeating all enemies in that level = ruining the flow (2) The 3 secret stars (per level) are sometimes hidden in such a way, that you need to consume or waste so much time to find your way to them, while not always being able to find a clue = trial-and-error involved
- I didn't find any targets in Time Attack Mode. so it's basically just: finish all levels again and get your achievement. You could try to finish a level very fast, but what's the point? Well, maybe to get a nice ranking in the Steam leaderboards.
7/10

pros: weapon designs, high frames per second, some maps are large and expansive (sadly there is no radar/map screen available), free download
cons: no option for borderless windowed mode even in the launch options (
), as of this review there are only 2 servers on the server list that I can practically join (several are psw locked, and the rest have 200+ping) but there is an alternate way to search for servers (
), missing textures, the game doesn't teach you the differences between selectable classes (except in a random hint box that pops up for 3 seconds before the round starts), crappy hitbox, extremely difficult to spot an enemy from mid-long range (partly due to dated visuals), the game does not detect your steam name so it assigns you as "neotokyonoob" by default, no chatbox - you have to scroll through the console menu, no soundtrack in-game but one is available online (
)
Overall, after playing this stand-alone version, it just about reminds me of how exactly Neotokyo was years ago during the golden age of Half-Life source mods (Dystopia, Age of Chivalry, Zombie Panic: Source, and many others).  It's almost as if everything was copied and pasted into the stand-alone source engine, missing an opportunity to really upgrade the visuals and the whole gameplay experience. I remember playing the original mod for several hours then deciding to eventually move on to better source mods, and sadly this new stand-alone version still has not convinced me otherwise. "No More Room In Hell" stand-alone had a terrible launch a few months ago (nov 2013) with a severe lack of available servers and it seems Neotokyo didn't learn from those mistakes. I would like to point out "Fistful of Frags" as a great example on how to do a stand-alone source mod right - with tender loving care.
Neotokyo is a hit or miss.

